WEBVTT

NOTE Synthetic narration, macOS Samantha. Caption timing is approximate.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.779
Look at this road. The gap is on the left. Our program sends

00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:07.558
the picture to a model, gets a lane choice back, and uses that

00:00:07.558 --> 00:00:10.755
answer to steer. This is a recorded walkthrough of Pocket Pilot.

00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:15.215
A language model works with text. A vision language model can also read

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:19.264
images. An API is the interface our program uses to call a model.

00:00:19.264 --> 00:00:23.312
Those are separate ideas. The model supplies an answer, while our own code

00:00:23.312 --> 00:00:25.803
decides how and when to act on it.

00:00:26.208 --> 00:00:30.607
A Decisions request starts with the current state and a small question. Here,

00:00:30.607 --> 00:00:35.005
the state is a road image, and the question is: which lane is

00:00:35.005 --> 00:00:39.404
open? We supply the allowed answers, left, middle, and right. The response contains

00:00:39.404 --> 00:00:42.111
a typed answer that our program can inspect.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.409
Jev is a model made by TypeSafe. It reads text or structured JSON

00:00:47.409 --> 00:00:52.308
in this demo. Luna and Clef can also receive images. The Decisions API

00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:57.206
defines the interface, while OpenRouter sends our request to the selected provider. Changing

00:00:57.206 --> 00:01:00.221
the model can change accuracy, speed, and cost.

00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:05.185
A conventional model can explain the road, or generate a small JSON object

00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:09.735
using a schema. Decisions is useful when the answer types and options are

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:14.285
already known. Specialized models can score those options directly. But the API name

00:01:14.285 --> 00:01:18.835
alone does not guarantee faster or more accurate answers. We need to measure

00:01:18.835 --> 00:01:19.185
that.

00:01:19.594 --> 00:01:24.098
The three types answer different questions. Choice selects a label and returns probabilities

00:01:24.098 --> 00:01:28.603
over the labels. The yes probability type returns a number between zero and

00:01:28.603 --> 00:01:33.108
one. Score uses ordered levels and can return a fractional value. These are

00:01:33.108 --> 00:01:37.613
model estimates. A probability of one does not prove the answer is correct.

00:01:38.013 --> 00:01:42.127
Here is a real request from an earlier recorded call. It contains the

00:01:42.127 --> 00:01:46.241
model, the state, and the questions. The image is sent as a data

00:01:46.241 --> 00:01:50.355
URL. We shorten its bytes on screen, but students can download the exact

00:01:50.355 --> 00:01:52.254
request and inspect the full input.

00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:57.057
The response chose left. The other answers describe whether the middle lane is

00:01:57.057 --> 00:02:01.459
blocked and where the barrier appears. Only the lane answer controls this game.

00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:05.861
The timing shown here belongs to this one example. The benchmark slides summarize

00:02:05.861 --> 00:02:06.877
many separate calls.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.323
The new benchmark covers sixty three settings with three scenes per setting: a

00:02:12.323 --> 00:02:17.366
hundred and eighty nine attempts. We test all supported models, three image sizes,

00:02:17.366 --> 00:02:22.409
and one, two, or three questions. The table shows accuracy, errors, median and

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:27.453
mean time, the observed range, reported tokens, and cost. Use the controls to

00:02:27.453 --> 00:02:29.780
compare the same setting across models.

00:02:30.196 --> 00:02:34.554
For the one question, four hundred and twenty pixel image, Luna's median was

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:38.911
about point six seconds. The two chat models were about one point six

00:02:38.911 --> 00:02:43.269
seconds. With JSON, Jev and Luna were both around half a second. Across

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:47.626
the complete experiment, there were three wrong lane choices and eighteen request size

00:02:47.626 --> 00:02:50.643
errors. Speed is only one part of the result.

00:02:51.043 --> 00:02:56.705
For the Sustainability Lab, I would start with field photo quality: is an

00:02:56.705 --> 00:03:02.367
image usable, blurred, or obstructed? Students can check those labels themselves. Other possibilities

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:08.028
include visible smoke triage, solar panel inspection, and waste audits. These are proposed

00:03:08.028 --> 00:03:13.255
applications. A smoke image cannot, by itself, tell us the particulate concentration.

00:03:13.668 --> 00:03:18.205
Some useful decisions need no image. We could combine sensor readings with technician

00:03:18.205 --> 00:03:22.742
notes, review building energy events, or route a research question to the right

00:03:22.742 --> 00:03:27.279
data tool. Simple rules should remain a baseline. A model is most interesting

00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:31.816
when context matters, and its suggested action should leave evidence for a person

00:03:31.816 --> 00:03:32.514
to review.

00:03:32.918 --> 00:03:36.848
This sensor example is invented for teaching. A reading has stayed at zero

00:03:36.848 --> 00:03:40.778
while nearby measurements are higher. The question is what to check next. A

00:03:40.778 --> 00:03:44.707
typed answer could put the case in a review queue. It should not

00:03:44.707 --> 00:03:48.335
silently delete the measurement or claim that it has diagnosed the cause.

00:03:48.752 --> 00:03:53.690
Choose a task with labels we can check. Hold out examples before tuning,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:58.629
compare simple rules with conventional models and Decisions, and report mistakes as well

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:03.568
as speed and cost. Keep a review option for ambiguous cases. Then use

00:04:03.568 --> 00:04:08.127
Pocket Pilot to inspect every request, response, image, and timing for yourself.
